-- Fix structurally-impossible 0%/100% on-time rate on the Payments page
-- (backlog follow-up to 20261001000000_widen_payment_behaviour_window.sql).
--
-- WHAT: for the 32 "watch"/"at_risk"/"distressed" demo customers (not
-- "healthy" -- see WHY), replaces their 8 seeded days_early_late values with
-- new ones that are still deterministic and still compute to the SAME
-- intended persona health (verified offline against the real
-- analyse-payment-behaviour.ts logic, ported 1:1 to Python for generation --
-- see tests/payments/intended.json / verify-payments.mjs to re-check after
-- applying), but are no longer uniformly one sign across all 8 transactions.
--
-- WHY: found 2026-10-02 reviewing the live Payments page -- the On-time
-- column only ever showed 0% or 100%, for every customer, no matter the
-- window width. Root cause: every demo customer's 8 seeded transactions
-- were uniformly early (healthy bucket) or uniformly late (watch/at_risk/
-- distressed buckets) -- never a mix -- so On-time (days_early_late <= 0,
-- computed over the current 90-day window by v_payment_behaviour_current)
-- was mathematically forced to 0% or 100% regardless of how wide that
-- window is. The Oct 1 migration fixed a different, real problem (too few
-- transactions per window for stddev/volatility to mean anything) but could
-- not have fixed this one, because it isn't a window-width problem.
--
-- SCOPE: the "healthy" bucket (27 customers) is untouched -- a customer
-- who legitimately always pays on time correctly shows 100% on-time, that
-- is not a bug. Of the remaining 32:
--   - all 10 "watch" customers ("consistently late but stable") now show a
--     genuine mixed on-time rate in their current window (1 of their most
--     recent 3 payments lands on-time/early, verified against
--     analyse-payment-trend.ts's current_on_time_rate math).
--   - the 22 "at_risk"/"distressed" customers (actively, sharply
--     deteriorating -- confirmed via exhaustive search up to +/-18 days of
--     jitter) cannot show a mixed current-window rate without their trend
--     classification flipping away from "deteriorating" and their health
--     away from "at_risk" -- i.e. a customer whose payment timing is
--     genuinely worsening fast would not realistically have a recent
--     on-time payment either. Their 0% on-time stands as correct, not as
--     the same seed-data artifact -- each still got light non-monotonic
--     jitter (not a smooth ramp) so the day-to-day numbers aren't
--     suspiciously tidy, without changing the classification outcome.
--
-- HOW: payment_date, amount_paid, and the Net-30/Net-45 terms convention
-- per persona bucket are all unchanged from
-- 20260929010000_individualize_payment_transactions.sql -- only
-- days_early_late (and the invoice_date/invoice_due_date/days_to_pay/
-- on_time fields derived from it) were regenerated. demo_days_offset is
-- unchanged, so fn_reset_demo_payment_dates() keeps re-anchoring these rows
-- correctly on every future demo reset.
--
-- Scope: payment_transactions timing columns only, for exactly the 32
-- customer_ids deleted/reinserted below. customers.current_exposure,
-- customers.net_terms, and all other customers' rows are untouched.

DELETE FROM payment_transactions WHERE customer_id IN (
